[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"change-to-common-logarithms","label":"Change to the given logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-to-common-logarithms":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2 real values","reason":"Set t=log_b x. Then log_a x=t log_a b, so t^2=1 because log_a b≠0. Thus x=b or 1/b, both positive and distinct since b≠1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046","cnm_h043","cnm_074"],"id":"common-base-square","label":"Use one logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-square":["cnm_h045","cnm_220","cnm_h048"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_220","cnm_h048","cnm_h046","cnm_h043","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1+log(3)/log(2); the full solution set is {1, log_2(6)}.","reason":"With u=2^x, the equation becomes (u-2)(u-6)=0. Thus x=1 or x=log_2(6)=1+log(3)/log(2), so D is a solution.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"],"id":"quadratic-exponential-substitution","label":"Substitute for the repeated power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-exponential-substitution":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["math:quadratic-exponential-substitution","cnm_224","cnm_h048","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","math:real-exponent-laws","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 3pq/(1+3pq)","reason":"The givens imply ln(3)=3p*ln(2) and ln(5)=3pq*ln(2). Thus ln(10)=(1+3pq)ln(2), giving log_10(5)=3pq/(1+3pq).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"common-base-two","label":"Rewrite the logarithms in base 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-two":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, none of these","reason":"For r=(log_a x)/(log_b x)=log_a b, the equation becomes 4r²-8r+3=0. Thus r=1/2 or 3/2, equivalently a=b² or b²=a³. No listed statement gives this full condition.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_160"],"id":"common-base-and-factor","label":"Find the complete condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-and-factor":["cnm_h045","cnm_224","cnm_h013"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_224","cnm_h013","cnm_h043","cnm_h044","cnm_160"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (1/log₆₀ x)","reason":"The sum is log_x 3+log_x 4+log_x 5=log_x 60=1/log₆₀ x. The assumptions ensure every logarithm and reciprocal used is defined.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"use-a-common-logarithm-base","label":"Use a common logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-a-common-logarithm-base":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 13 square root of 3","reason":"For t = log_y(x), t + 1/t = 10/3 gives t = 3 or 1/3. Thus one variable is the cube of the other. Their values are 2 square root of 3 and 24 square root of 3, in either order, so their mean is 13 square root of 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_220","cnm_171"],"id":"reciprocal-logarithms","label":"Solve for the logarithm ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-logarithms":["cnm_h045","cnm_h043","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h043","cnm_224","cnm_220","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: -1","reason":"We have a_n = log base 2002 of n. Therefore b-c = log base 2002 of [(2×3×4×5)/(10×11×12×13×14)] = log base 2002 of (1/2002) = -1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"combine-common-base-logarithms","label":"Rewrite every term with one base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"combine-common-base-logarithms":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 12","reason":"Set t=ln x. The quadratic is 8t²-(7ln m+6ln n)t-2013ln m ln n=0 and has two real roots. Hence the product K of the x-solutions satisfies K⁸=m⁷n⁶. For any prime dividing m, its exponents obey 7u+6v=8w with u>0; the smallest possible w is 4, attained at (u,v)=(2,3). Therefore K≥2⁴=16. Equality forces m=4 and n=8, so m+n=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h046","cnm_h048","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"logarithmic-root-sum-and-prime-exponents","label":"Use the sum of logarithmic roots and prime exponents","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-root-sum-and-prime-exponents":["cnm_h045","cnm_226","cnm_223","math:prime-factorization","math:extremal-principle"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_226","cnm_223","math:prime-factorization","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h044","cnm_h046","cnm_h048","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, a = 24","reason":"For a > 0 and a ≠ 1, the left side equals log_a 2 + log_a 3 + log_a 4 = log_a 24. Thus log_a 24 = 1, which gives a = 24 and satisfies the original domain restrictions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"reciprocal-change-of-base","label":"Use a common logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-change-of-base":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 31, with x = 4/27","reason":"Change of base gives 2 ln(2x) = 3 ln(3x), hence ln x = 2 ln 2 - 3 ln 3 = ln(4/27). Therefore x = 4/27, which satisfies the stated domain restrictions, and p + q = 4 + 27 = 31.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"change-base-and-solve","label":"Change to a common logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base-and-solve":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 6","reason":"The product becomes (ln 25 · ln 27)/(ln 3 · ln 5), since the factors involving 7 through 23 cancel. Using ln 25 = 2 ln 5 and ln 27 = 3 ln 3 gives 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"change-base-cancellation","label":"Change base and cancel","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base-cancellation":["cnm_h045","math:telescoping","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","math:telescoping","cnm_h044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 20","reason":"Set u = log₂x and v = log₂y. Change of base gives uv = 4, while xy = 64 gives u+v = 6. Therefore (log₂(x/y))² = (u-v)² = (u+v)²-4uv = 36-16 = 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-sum-and-product","label":"Use the sum and product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-sum-and-product":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 21,000","reason":"The first summand is k·log₅3, giving 210·log₅3. Each second summand is log₃5, giving 100·log₃5. Since log₅3·log₃5=1, the product is 210·100=21,000.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"change-base-and-sum","label":"Change bases and simplify the sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base-and-sum":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h204"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2","reason":"Set t = log₂5. The expression becomes (t+4)(t+3) - (t+5)(t+2) = (t²+7t+12) - (t²+7t+10) = 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"change-base-and-substitute","label":"Use one logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base-and-substitute":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 1","reason":"Let t=log_2023(x), a=log_2023(7), and b=log_2023(289). Since a+b=1, the equation becomes (t+a)(t+b)=t+1, or t^2=1-ab. Both real roots are admissible: the forbidden values -a, -b, and -1 are not roots. Their sum is zero, so log_2023(x1x2)=0 and x1x2=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"opposite-logarithmic-roots","label":"Find opposite logarithmic roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"opposite-logarithmic-roots":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_220","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_220","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, approximately 1.47","reason":"Since 135=27·5, the equation gives 3^x=5. Therefore x=log 5/log 3=(1-0.3010)/0.4771≈1.4651, or 1.47.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h044","math:real-exponent-laws"],"id":"logarithm-change-of-base","label":"Simplify the power, then use logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithm-change-of-base":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","math:real-exponent-laws"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (10/7)","reason":"log₅10=1/log₁₀5=1/(1-log₁₀2)≈1/0.699≈1.4306. Among the choices, 10/7≈1.4286 is closest.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_023"],"id":"base-ten","label":"Change to base 10","methodDirectNodeIds":{"base-ten":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043","cnm_023"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 1","reason":"Each term 1/log_j(100!) equals log_(100!)(j). Their sum is log_(100!)(2·3·...·100)=log_(100!)(100!)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1998_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","math:factorial-notation"],"id":"m1","label":"Change to a common logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043","math:factorial-notation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: pi","reason":"On the real domain implicit in log_a b, the circumference equation becomes 4log_10(b)=2pi times 2log_10(a). Hence log_a b=log_10(b)/log_10(a)=pi.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_p190"],"id":"2008_AMC_12B_Q14_m1","label":"Use circumference and change base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2008_AMC_12B_Q14_m1":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_h043","cnm_p190"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 13, with n = 256","reason":"Let t=log₄(n)>0. Then log₁₆(n)=t/2, so log₂(t)-1=(1/2)log₂(t). Hence log₂(t)=2, t=4, and n=4⁴=256. Its digit sum is 2+5+6=13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042"],"id":"change-to-base-two","label":"Reduce to a linear logarithmic equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-to-base-two":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h048","cnm_h028"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_042","cnm_h048","cnm_h028"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-outer-logarithms","label":"Remove the outer logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-outer-logarithms":["cnm_h045","cnm_h048","cnm_h028","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h048","cnm_h028","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: sqrt(log_2 3) + sqrt(log_3 2)","reason":"Let a = log_2 3 > 0. Then log_3 2 = 1/a, so the radicand is 2 + a + 1/a = (sqrt(a) + sqrt(1/a))^2. Both summands are positive, giving D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reciprocal-logs-and-perfect-square","label":"Recognize a perfect square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-logs-and-perfect-square":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_148","cnm_169"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h044","cnm_h045","cnm_148","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, x=576","reason":"Positive edge lengths require x>1. Dividing abc=2(ab+ac+bc) by 2abc gives 1/2=1/a+1/b+1/c=log_x 2+log_x 3+log_x 4=log_x 24. Therefore √x=24, so x=576.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"reciprocal-logarithms","label":"Reciprocal edge lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-logarithms":["cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h043"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_h048"],"id":"common-base-parameter","label":"Common-base parameter","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-parameter":["cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_042","cnm_h048","cnm_p161","cnm_p163","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: log(s/P)/log(1+k), under the required real-logarithm conditions","reason":"When P is nonzero, s/P>0, 1+k>0, and k is nonzero, (1+k)^n=s/P gives n*log(1+k)=log(s/P), yielding A.","status":"ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["The necessary parameter restrictions are unstated. In particular, if k=0 and P=s is nonzero, every n satisfies the original equation and the keyed quotient is undefined.","A unique real logarithmic solution requires P!=0, s/P>0, 1+k>0 and k!=0. If k=0 and P=s!=0 every n works."],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"define-log-and-change-base","label":"Invert by a logarithm","methodDirectNodeIds":{"define-log-and-change-base":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. All positive x≠1","reason":"For x>0 and x≠1, change of base gives (ln x/ln 3)(ln 5/ln x)=ln 5/ln 3=log₃5. The excluded value x=1 cannot be a logarithm base.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"change-logarithm-base","label":"Apply change of base","nodeIds":["cnm_h045"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046"],"id":"solution:change-logarithm-base:39f8abc382d9","label":"Change the logarithm base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-logarithm-base":["cnm_h045"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: x=0.5","reason":"Since 9^(x+2)=81*9^x, the equation gives 80*9^x=240 and therefore 9^x=3=9^(1/2). Thus x=1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-inversion","label":"Recover the exponent with logs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-inversion":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 9","reason":"On the original logarithm domain, the left side equals log₃y and the right side is 2. Thus log₃y=2 and y=9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q45","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h046"],"id":"common-base-cancellation","label":"Method 1: Change to a common base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-base-cancellation":["cnm_h045","cnm_h043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h043","cnm_h046"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 5/3","reason":"8^x=32 becomes 2^(3x)=2^5. Since powers of 2 are injective, 3x=5 and x=5/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"logarithmic-exponent","label":"Evaluate the exponent using logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"logarithmic-exponent":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2b/3","reason":"a=log_2(15^2)/log_2(8)=2log_2(15)/3=2b/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"change-base","label":"Change to base 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","cnm_h044","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, y=5","reason":"The product is (log 3/log 2)(log 4/log 3)...(log 32/log 31)=log 32/log 2=log_2 32=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043"],"id":"change-base-and-cancel","label":"Change to a common base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"change-base-and-cancel":["cnm_h045","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h045","math:telescoping","cnm_h043"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 7/2","reason":"For j=1,...,124, x_j=log(j+4)/log(j+3). Their product telescopes to log(128)/log(4)=7/2. Equivalently, if P is the product, then 4^P=128.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Retaining the logarithmic route does not mean logarithms or explicit telescoping are universally required; the power-chaining alternative remains mathematically valid."],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"telescoping-logarithms","label":"Change base and cancel","nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","math:telescoping"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_157"],"id":"solution:telescoping-logarithms:d8dd8ea9ea06","label":"Expose cancellation with logarithms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"telescoping-logarithms":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h045","math:telescoping","cnm_157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:06c6f14574d9ae49d87a5778"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 8/7","reason":"Dividing 7^(x+7)=8^x by 7^x gives 7^7=(8/7)^x. By the definition of logarithm, x=log_(8/7)(7^7), so b=8/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h045"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"take-logarithms","label":"Logarithms an